// F188 (stage 8 step 3): `abs` is the first polymorphic builtin whose type rule comes from its
// declarations rather than from a hand-written arm. Its four `builtin fn` lines were already
// there, documenting what the arm checked; the arm is gone and the resolver checks them.
//
// **Nothing in the runtime changed.** `abs` is still dispatched by its builtin id -- the
// resolver deliberately does not record a `builtin fn` on `call_fn`, because a builtin has no
// body and `interpreter.c` consults `call_fn` first. So this is a type-checking change only,
// which is what makes the remaining ~50 conversions cheap.
fn main() {
    print(abs(-3))
    print(abs(-2.5))
    print(abs(-7n))
    print(abs(-1.50d))
    print(abs(0))
}
